%I A401339 #9 Nov 08 2026 09:26:42
%S A401339 10,15,18,20,30,40,45,50,60,70,80,90,111,120,126,222,240,250,285,333,
%T A401339 360,444,480,555,666,750,777,888,999,1041,1185,1395,1443,1554,1665,
%U A401339 1893,1998,2082,2250,2331,2528,2757,2886,3885,4250,4662,4995,6055,6993,7200,7245,10101
%N A401339 Primitive terms of A343550.
%C A401339 If m is a term of A343550 then so is m*10^i, i > 0; so, a term m of A343550 is called primitive if there is no term k of A343550 such that m = 10*k.
%e A401339 15 is a term because 105/15 = 7.
%e A401339 40 is a term because 400/40 = 10 and 4 is not a term of A343550.
%e A401339 150 is not a term although 10500/150 = 70 but 150 = 10*15 and 15 is a term of A343550.
%o A401339 (PARI) isok(k) = if (k>=10, my(d=digits(k), kk = fromdigits(vector(2*#d-1, i, if (i%2, d[i\2+1])))); (kk % k) == 0); \\ A343550
%o A401339 isprimitive(k) = if (isok(k), my(p=valuation(k, 10)); if (p, forstep(i=p, 1, -1, if (isok(k/10^i), return(0)))); 1); \\ _Michel Marcus_, Nov 08 2026
%Y A401339 Subsequence of A343550.
%K A401339 nonn,base,new
%O A401339 1,1
%A A401339 _Bernard Schott_, Oct 21 2026
